{"type":"assistant","uuid":"037fda9f-e344-44cd-85dd-2079ae83bce2","session_id":"13c061ab-458d-404f-9d5d-caa688794da3","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"037fda9f-e344-44cd-85dd-2079ae83bce2","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now let me check on the exploration agents. But first, let me think about the bug more carefully.\n\nThe bug says \"The url of a video shared via 'Copy Url' is invalid.\" The example shows:\n```\nhttps://www.youtube.com/https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCKvfzCyVQPe2IYg4PkugvWQ\n```\n\nThis is a URL that:1. Starts with `https://www.youtube.com/`\n2. Is followed by another URL `https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCKvfzCyVQPe2IYg4PkugvWQ`3. The inner URL is a CHANNEL URL, not a video URL\n\nThis is clearly not coming from `getVideoUrl()` which returns `https://youtu.be/{id}`. So where could this come from?\n\nWait, could this be from `YoutubeChannelLinkHandlerFactory.getInstance().getUrl(getId())`?\n\n\nThe duplicated domain prefix is the real clue here—if `getId()` somehow returns a full URL like `https://www.youtube.com/channel/...`, then `getUrl()` would prepend another domain, creating exactly this malformed result. The question is how the channel ID field gets populated with a complete URL in the first place, likely through one of the YouTubeChannel constructors being called with the wrong data.\n\nLooking at the constructor chain, I'm seeing that `extractor.getUploaderUrl()` from NewPipe is being passed as the channel ID, but this method might be returning a full URL rather than just the ID string. NewPipe's StreamExtractor appears to be providing the uploader's complete URL instead of extracting just the channel identifier, which then gets treated as an ID and passed through the URL generation method. The bug is that the video URL is being replaced with the channel URL entirely, not just concatenated. Looking at the `YouTubeChannel` constructor being called with `extractor.getUploaderUrl()` as the channel ID, if that method returns a full URL instead of just an ID, then `getChannelUrl()` would try to build a URL from that, creating the malformed result with duplicated scheme and authority components. the channel ID.\n\nSo the real issue is that `extractor.getUploaderUrl()` returns a full URL when it should return just an ID. If I can extract the actual channel ID from that URL in the `getDetails` method, it would fix the malformed channel URL. Though I'm still puzzled about how this causes the video URL duplication—the channel URL being used as the video ID seems like a separate problem.\n\nLet me trace through `VideoId.create` to see if there's something I'm missing about how the IDs are being constructed. The bug report shows a duplicated YouTube URL, which suggests the URL is being concatenated with itself somewhere—either by double-prefixing it or concatenating the channel URL twice. I need to trace through where this duplication could occur, particularly in how the video object's URL field is being set or passed around.\n\nLooking at the actual video URL construction, if the `id` field somehow contains a full URL like `https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCKvfzCyVQPe2IYg4PkugvWQ` instead of just the channel identifier, then `getVideoUrl()` would produce the malformed output we're seeing. This means the issue likely stems from how the `YouTubeVideo` object is initialized—the `id` field is being set to a complete channel URL rather than the raw channel ID. I'm tracing through the `getDetails` method to see how the video is constructed—it creates a `YouTubeVideo` object using the extractor's ID, name, description, length, and a `YouTubeChannel` initialized with the uploader's URL and name.\n\nThe key issue I'm identifying is that `extractor.getUploaderUrl()` likely returns a full URL string like `https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCKvfzCyVQPe2IYg4PkugvWQ`, which then gets stored as the channel's `id` field. When `getChannelUrl()` is called later, it tries to construct a URL by prepending `https://www.youtube.com/` to this already-complete URL, resulting in the malformed output with the duplicated protocol and domain.\n\nHowever, there's still a separate puzzle: if the channel `id` contains a full URL, the `YouTubeVideo` itself should still have its own `id` field (the actual video ID like `7MoipvBda_8`), so `getVideoUrl()` should return something like `https://youtu.be/7MoipvBda_8`. The fact that it's returning a malformed channel URL instead suggests something else is overwriting or corrupting the video's `id` field.\n\nI should investigate whether there's an issue in how `NewPipeExtractor` constructs these objects, or if there's a recent code change that introduced this regression. Let me check the exploration agents and the git history to trace when this started.\n","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0}}}
